export const debate = {"id":"winger-dillahunty-resurrection-unreasonable-2019","number":"69","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Mike Winger vs Matt Dillahunty: Is Belief in the Resurrection Unreasonable?","label":"Resurrection burden and history","date":"2026-08-27","duration":"1 hr 58 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2FGgkubhZM","motion":"Is belief in Jesus' resurrection reasonable given the historical claims, testimonial sources, rival explanations, and evidential burden for supernatural causation?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Winger builds a cumulative historical case for resurrection, while Dillahunty argues that transmitted reports and unresolved causal gaps justify withholding belief.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"why I think it's reasonable to believe Jesus rose","context":"Winger introduces a two-part method that first identifies likely historical facts and then compares explanations for their conjunction."},"con":{"text":"the quantity and quality of evidence for a claim","context":"Dillahunty clarifies that extraordinary evidence concerns proportionate evidential strength, rather than a recursive demand for miraculous supporting claims."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Resurrection defense","speaker":"Mike Winger","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Skeptical burden test","speaker":"Matt Dillahunty","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":80,"con":86},"sections":[{"sectionId":"historical-fact-foundation","title":"Historical facts and source reliability","timebox":"7:30–60:41","score":{"pro":84,"con":79},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-roman-crucifixion-death","time":"7:30","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Jesus' death by Roman crucifixion under Pontius Pilate is presented as a securely supported historical premise for evaluating the resurrection claim.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move identifies a necessary historical premise with a clear agent, official setting, named scholarly support, and asserted source multiplicity, making its relevance to any bodily-resurrection inference immediate and exceptionally easy to recover. Principal limitation: The appeal to scholarly agreement and eleven independent sources remains compressed because neither the independence criteria nor the evidential contribution of those sources is demonstrated within the selected material. Live burden: The argument must distinguish consensus as useful orientation from the underlying evidence and explain why dependence, dating, genre, or transmission concerns do not materially weaken the death premise. Locked score: The strong band recognizes coherent presentation, substantial historical plausibility, and indispensable burden contact while retaining a modest deduction for unsupported numerical and independence claims that carry evidential weight.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-late-dependent-sources-undermine-facts","time":"40:58","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Late, noncontemporary, source-dependent accounts with unresolved inconsistencies do not securely transform the proposed minimal-facts list into established historical facts.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply targets the cumulative case at its foundation by identifying dating, dependence, and inconsistency as evidential variables that can weaken confidence before any best-explanation comparison begins. Principal limitation: The selected presentation ranges broadly across historical premises without documenting the severity and relevance of each alleged problem for each individual fact, whose support and source history may differ substantially. Live burden: The con side must provide itemized source criticism showing which claims depend on which texts, what inconsistencies matter, and how much confidence should be reduced rather than treating all premises uniformly. Locked score: The strong band reflects central relevance, appropriately nonabsolute force, and a credible source-sufficiency challenge, while acknowledging that the reply remains only partial without detailed evidential accounting.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-burial-empty-tomb","time":"8:29","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Jesus was probably privately buried and his tomb later found empty, with multiple attestation, women witnesses, Joseph, and archaeology offered cumulatively.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The claim is explicitly probabilistic and candidly acknowledges weaker scholarly support, while assembling several concrete considerations for private burial and an empty tomb instead of treating either proposition as self-authenticating. Principal limitation: Multiple attestation, Joseph of Arimathea, women witnesses, and archaeological compatibility are compressed into a cumulative list without adequately establishing source independence or the discriminating force of each consideration. Live burden: The case must show why these considerations jointly favor the proposed burial and discovery over literary development, dependent transmission, uncertain burial practice, or other accounts compatible with the surviving reports. Locked score: The strong band rewards qualification, specificity, and central relevance, while reflecting that the most consequential reliability warrants remain asserted more briefly than the historical conclusion requires.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-no-contemporary-crucifixion-verification","time":"59:57","role":"Major direct reply","words":"The absence of contemporary verification and expected independent reporting for conspicuous associated events weakens confidence in the crucifixion narrative source.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly tests the quality of historical verification and identifies potentially expected external reporting, thereby challenging whether later narrative sources alone justify the confidence assigned to the foundational account. Principal limitation: Absence of surviving contemporary reports can lower confidence without making the narrative unverifiable, and the broader characterization exceeds what the selected missing-evidence considerations independently establish. Live burden: The skeptical case must defend expectations of preservation and reporting, distinguish the crucifixion from associated extraordinary events, and weigh later or indirect evidence rather than treating contemporaneity as a categorical requirement. Locked score: The strong band recognizes relevant source scrutiny and meaningful contact with the historical foundation, while reflecting overbreadth and the limited inferential force of documentary absence without a developed expectation model.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"testimony-and-corroboration","title":"Testimony, sincerity, and corroboration","timebox":"14:10–72:07","score":{"pro":75,"con":91},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-disciple-sincerity-transformation","time":"14:10","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Transformation, persecution, and costly commitment support the conclusion that Peter, James, Paul, and other disciples sincerely believed they encountered the risen Jesus.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move draws a restrained and logically relevant inference from costly commitment to sincerity, identifies particularly supported individuals, and avoids claiming that willingness to suffer automatically proves the resurrection belief true. Principal limitation: Its language sometimes expands from Peter, James, and Paul to the disciples generally, although the selected evidence provides materially stronger historical support for the named individuals than for the broader group. Live burden: The pro side must document who knowingly faced which costs for an appearance-based belief and preserve the distinction between sincere conviction, accurate memory, and veridical encounter. Locked score: The strong band reflects a coherent sincerity inference and useful corroborative role, while accounting for scope expansion and the limited capacity of transformation evidence to establish the underlying event independently.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-testimony-claims-not-supernatural-proof","time":"27:20","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Sincere testimony may establish that extraordinary experiences were reported without sufficiently establishing that the reports' supernatural interpretation is true.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply sharply distinguishes testimony, sincere experience, reported content, and supernatural truth, directly diagnosing the inferential gap between believing witnesses and an independently warranted resurrection conclusion. Principal limitation: The point establishes a necessary caution about testimonial sufficiency but does not quantify how much cumulative evidential value early, multiple, or costly testimony might nevertheless possess when combined with other historical considerations. Live burden: The skeptical case must assess the strongest testimonial configuration rather than testimony in abstraction and explain why dependence, transmission, and alternative experience mechanisms keep its total support below the required threshold. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise concepts, proportionate force, and decisive contact with a recurring pro inference, while leaving room for a fuller comparative account of testimony's positive but limited contribution.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-local-detail-corroboration","time":"54:54","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Accurate Palestinian institutions, geography, customs, and local details cumulatively support the provenance and general historical reliability of Gospel reporting.","score":68,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies concrete local details rather than relying exclusively on general declarations of reliability, and those details can legitimately support geographical familiarity, cultural competence, or plausible provenance. Principal limitation: Accuracy about incidental settings does not strongly verify the central resurrection claims, yet the move slides from local knowledge toward broad historical trustworthiness without specifying that limitation. Live burden: The pro side must demonstrate a relevant correlation between verified background detail and reliable reporting of contested events, while addressing the possibility that accurate settings coexist with theological development or dependence. Locked score: The mixed band reflects genuine but subsidiary corroborative value and clear source contact, while recognizing that the reply only partially answers the decisive distinction between narrative competence and supernatural truth.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-paul-third-party-appearance-reports","time":"71:11","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Paul's third-party appearance reports do not show that the named witnesses personally attested as represented or that resurrection actually occurred.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply carefully separates Paul's own belief, his transmission of reports, the named witnesses' personal attestations, and the truth of the event, directly exposing several evidential steps often compressed together. Principal limitation: Third-party status limits directness but does not eliminate evidential value, especially if the tradition is early or Paul had meaningful access to relevant witnesses, considerations not fully weighed here. Live burden: The skeptical case must evaluate dating, transmission channels, opportunities for correction, and source dependence to explain why the reports remain insufficient despite whatever historical proximity they possess. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent precision, calibration, and decisive contact with the truth-support distinction, while allowing a modest qualification for underdeveloped assessment of indirect testimony's positive contribution.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"rival-explanations","title":"Resurrection and rival explanations","timebox":"12:30–50:42","score":{"pro":81,"con":87},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-appearance-experiences","time":"12:30","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Individual and group experiences interpreted as appearances of Jesus are historical data that a simple hallucination account does not adequately explain.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move usefully distinguishes post-death experiences from the further resurrection conclusion, names both individual and group reports, and supplies scholarly acknowledgment that appearance experiences belong within the historical data requiring explanation. Principal limitation: The argument moves too quickly from reported group experiences to the inadequacy of hallucination without establishing independent testimony, simultaneity, phenomenological similarity, or the precise psychological alternative being rejected. Live burden: It must demonstrate that the sources reliably preserve genuinely shared perceptual events and compare resurrection with socially reinforced, visionary, memorial, or transmission-based explanations at equivalent levels of specificity. Locked score: The strong band recognizes focused evidence, explanatory relevance, and qualified historical framing, while preserving a material deduction because occurrence of experiences does not by itself determine their external object or cause.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-known-natural-causes-outrank-resurrection","time":"32:37","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Known generators of false reports, including deception, legend, error, and exaggeration, remain better grounded than an unsupported resurrection mechanism.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply introduces relevant background knowledge by contrasting a poorly specified resurrection mechanism with familiar processes that generate inaccurate or exaggerated reports, thereby challenging the preferred explanation's initial comparative footing. Principal limitation: It does not examine how any particular natural process fits every proposed datum, and its use of dismissive mechanism language risks collapsing divine agency into caricature rather than evaluating the strongest formulation. Live burden: The skeptical comparison must connect identified natural mechanisms to source dependence, appearance traditions, empty-tomb reporting, and movement development with enough detail to demonstrate cumulative adequacy rather than mere availability. Locked score: The strong band recognizes legitimate prior-weight reasoning and direct contact with explanatory viability, while retaining deductions for rhetorical compression and the absence of a datum-by-datum natural account.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-resurrection-best-explanation","time":"20:23","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Resurrection is presented as explaining the cumulative data better than conspiracy, hallucination, legend, or combinations of those natural alternatives.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move squarely addresses the case's central comparative burden by naming multiple natural alternatives and evaluating resurrection as a unified explanation of the proposed historical data rather than as an isolated possibility. Principal limitation: The criteria for explanatory superiority remain partly implicit, and the separate or combined alternatives receive compressed treatment that does not match the specificity granted to the preferred explanation. Live burden: The comparison must define scope, power, plausibility, evidential fit, and auxiliary assumptions, then apply those criteria consistently to resurrection, natural combinations, and unresolved uncertainty. Locked score: The strong band recognizes substantial relevance and a coherent comparative ambition, while retaining a significant deduction because dismissing selected rivals cannot establish resurrection unless the data and candidate set are sufficiently secure.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-elimination-does-not-establish-resurrection","time":"47:46","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Eliminating selected natural alternatives cannot establish resurrection under incomplete information, so unresolved possibilities and insufficient evidence warrant uncertainty.","score":91,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply decisively separates failure of selected alternatives from positive support for resurrection and uses incomplete information to justify uncertainty without claiming that the event is impossible or demonstrably false. Principal limitation: Its strongest verbal rejection is firmer than the illustrative underdetermination argument alone establishes, because sufficiently discriminating evidence could in principle support an explanation despite an incompletely enumerated candidate set. Live burden: The skeptical position must show that the actual record remains materially underdetermined, not merely that unknown explanations are logically possible, and identify the evidential deficits sustaining that judgment. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent conceptual precision, direct burden contact, and proportionate nonacceptance, with only a limited deduction for rhetoric that occasionally exceeds the carefully qualified epistemic conclusion.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"supernatural-causation","title":"Event evidence and supernatural causation","timebox":"17:28–82:52","score":{"pro":73,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-religious-context-divine-cause","time":"17:28","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"If Jesus rose, his religious, miraculous, and prophetic context is offered as evidence that the event should be understood as divine action.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move recognizes that establishing an unusual return to life and identifying God as its cause are distinct burdens, then attempts the indispensable causal bridge through the event's surrounding religious and prophetic context. Principal limitation: Religious meaning, miracle traditions, and prophecy are grouped together without demonstrating their independent reliability or showing why contextual fit strongly discriminates divine action from human interpretation, coincidence, or later narrative formation. Live burden: The argument must specify which contextual facts are independently established, how strongly they predict divine intervention, and why the proposed agent is better supported than an unspecified cause. Locked score: The strong band rewards direct contact with a necessary causal question and an explicit conditional qualification, while reflecting substantial underdevelopment in the inference from meaningful context to divine agency.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-supernatural-candidate-needs-support","time":"29:25","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Resurrection cannot become the best explanation merely by contextual labeling; supernatural interaction and causal eligibility require independent evidential support.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly challenges the eligibility of resurrection as an explanatory candidate and correctly refuses to let religious context substitute automatically for evidence concerning possibility, interaction, and causal attribution. Principal limitation: Requiring prior evidence that the supernatural is real can sound categorically stronger than necessary, because a hypothesis might receive some support through the very observations it is introduced to explain. Live burden: The con side must formulate a noncircular threshold that permits hypothesis evaluation while demanding enough independent or discriminating evidence to distinguish supernatural causation from an unsupported label. Locked score: The strong band rewards direct responsiveness and an important causal safeguard, while reflecting compression between modal possibility and actual interaction and some overstatement in treating preliminary proof as a prerequisite.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-bodily-resurrection-meaning","time":"66:34","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Pauline language and Jewish historical concepts are offered to show that the earliest resurrection proclamation concerned Jesus' body, not merely a spectral experience.","score":70,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move supplies relevant semantic and cultural material for clarifying what the earliest proclamation meant, thereby preventing the dispute from equivocating between bodily resurrection and a merely spiritual survival claim. Principal limitation: Establishing the content of Paul's belief does not establish its truth, and the broad appeal to Jewish mentality compresses potentially diverse concepts into a uniform interpretive conclusion. Live burden: The pro side must substantiate the linguistic and historical generalization, acknowledge plausible semantic alternatives, and then provide independent warrant that the bodily event described actually occurred. Locked score: The mixed band reflects useful definitional clarification and recoverable content, while recognizing that the reply does not answer the opponent's decisive distinction between what an early claimant meant and what the evidence establishes.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-event-confirmation-not-divine-cause","time":"81:48","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Even direct confirmation that Jesus returned to life would not independently identify a supernatural mechanism or God as the event's cause.","score":94,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply grants that specified observations could establish Jesus alive again while rigorously separating event confirmation from the additional questions of mechanism and agency, making the causal gap unmistakable. Principal limitation: The distinction does not show that religious context is evidentially irrelevant; sufficiently specific prior claims or independently supported context could alter the comparative probability of candidate causes. Live burden: The con side must assess the actual contextual evidence and demonstrate that it lacks enough independence, specificity, or predictive force to identify divine action rather than merely insisting that another inference is required. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional conceptual clarity, charitable concession, and direct engagement with a necessary causal bridge, while preserving a narrow opening for contextual evidence to contribute.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"evidential-burden","title":"Extraordinary claims and evidential burden","timebox":"58:01–89:56","score":{"pro":82,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-two-part-standard-defense","time":"58:01","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Reasonably established nonmiraculous facts may be assessed before resurrection is compared as their best explanation, without presupposing that conclusion.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply clearly separates historical-premise assessment from explanatory comparison and correctly observes that supporting evidence need not prove a conclusion before it can be considered, thereby resisting a potentially circular threshold. Principal limitation: Defending the admissibility and sequence of evidence does not establish that the selected premises are sufficiently reliable or that resurrection ultimately satisfies the opponent's proportionate evidential standard. Live burden: The pro side must carry the valid two-part framework through a symmetrical comparison that incorporates background plausibility, source uncertainty, combined alternatives, and the distinct inference to divine agency. Locked score: The very strong band reflects an exceptionally clear and justified reframing that preserves the opponent's legitimate demand for evidence, while leaving the framework's substantive historical and causal execution unresolved.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-extraordinary-resurrection-needs-sufficient-support","time":"83:37","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Bodily resurrection is extraordinary, and the absence of physical, contemporary, and possibility-establishing evidence leaves its available support insufficient.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply addresses evidential sufficiency directly and identifies physical, contemporary, and possibility-related deficits rather than simply declaring extraordinary claims inadmissible or supernatural events impossible. Principal limitation: It does not fully defend why evidence must take those particular forms, and repeated absence language can understate the testimonial, traditional, and contextual evidence actually offered by the pro side. Live burden: The skeptical case must explain how evidential quantity, quality, independence, and background conflict jointly determine insufficiency without converting useful evidence categories into categorical prerequisites. Locked score: The strong band recognizes clear burden contact and concrete diagnostic content, while reflecting incomplete threshold justification and an occasionally overstated description of the positive evidence available.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-extraordinary-evidence-regress","time":"86:05","role":"Major direct reply","words":"If extraordinary evidence means further extraordinary claims needing equivalent support, the requirement generates an endless regress that excludes ordinary cumulative evidence.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies a genuine problem for a literal recursive demand and explains intelligibly why treating every extraordinary supporting item as another unsupported extraordinary claim would prevent evidential progress indefinitely. Principal limitation: The argument shifts between evidence that is unusual and evidence that is sufficiently strong, thereby targeting a weaker interpretation than the opponent's proportional quantity-and-quality standard. Live burden: The pro side must engage the strongest formulation by showing how ordinary evidence can cumulatively overcome low prior plausibility, rather than demonstrating that an unnecessary recursive formulation is defective. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct responsiveness, a coherent conditional regress, and important protection against categorical exclusion, while preserving substantial deductions for equivocation, overstatement, and limited charity toward the proportional standard.","tags":[{"label":"Equivocation","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/","context":"The regress depends on shifting extraordinary evidence from unusually strong support to an extraordinary claim that recursively demands further evidence."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-support-scales-with-extraordinariness","time":"88:28","role":"Major direct reply","words":"The quantity and quality of evidence needed for warranted belief should scale with extraordinariness and overcome conflicting background evidence.","score":93,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply precisely clarifies extraordinary evidence as proportionate quantity and quality, accepts that ordinary items may accumulate, and uses accessible comparisons to dissolve the regress-generating interpretation without excluding supernatural conclusions categorically. Principal limitation: The standard remains qualitative and does not supply a determinate method for measuring extraordinariness, dependence, background conflict, or the point at which cumulative historical evidence becomes sufficient. Live burden: The con side must apply the proportional principle transparently to the actual sources and explain why their aggregate support fails, rather than relying on the intuitive force of familiar examples alone. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional precision, charity, coherence, and decisive responsiveness, with only a modest residual burden concerning operational application.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"reasonableness-and-suspension","title":"Reasonable belief and suspension of judgment","timebox":"5:25–117:01","score":{"pro":85,"con":92},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-cumulative-reasonableness-method","time":"5:25","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Reasonable resurrection belief can arise from first establishing likely historical facts and then cumulatively comparing the explanations offered for those facts.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The two-stage framework is lucid, carefully qualified, and directly connected to the motion, giving the subsequent historical claims and explanatory comparisons an intelligible argumentative destination rather than presenting them as disconnected observations. Principal limitation: Announcing a sound comparative method does not itself establish that the selected facts are reliable, mutually independent, or collectively more probable under resurrection than under natural or unresolved alternatives. Live burden: The case must therefore defend each material datum, specify comparative explanatory criteria, and show that their cumulative interaction supplies substantially more warrant than the individual claims considered separately. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent relevance, recoverable structure, and proportionate possibility language, while preserving a meaningful evidential gap between an appropriate method and its successful execution.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-unmet-burden-permits-nonacceptance","time":"58:42","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Finding resurrection insufficiently supported permits nonacceptance without implying impossibility, nonoccurrence, or a presupposition against every supernatural event.","score":95,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply precisely distinguishes possibility, occurrence, burden satisfaction, and belief, showing that withholding acceptance after an unmet burden neither assumes naturalism nor establishes that resurrection did not happen. Principal limitation: The conceptual distinction is exceptionally strong, but applying it still depends on the preceding empirical assessment that the offered evidence actually remains insufficient under an appropriate standard. Live burden: The con side must continue grounding suspension in specific source, explanatory, and causal deficits so that nonacceptance follows from the record rather than from a merely formal allocation of burden. Locked score: The exceptional band reflects outstanding clarity, calibration, charity, and direct resolution of the circularity accusation, while recognizing that even a correct burden principle requires case-specific evidential support.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-modern-investigability","time":"116:36","role":"Supporting reply","words":"First-century material and accumulated historical research permit thoughtful modern investigation of Jesus' death and the subsequent resurrection claims.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply correctly resists the suggestion that ancient claims are categorically beyond assessment and reconnects accumulated scholarship and surviving first-century material to the possibility of responsible modern historical investigation. Principal limitation: The claim that present investigators are better placed than anyone else overgeneralizes across lost evidence, temporal distance, preservation, access, and methodological disagreement, while investigability alone does not establish evidential sufficiency. Live burden: The pro side must show specifically how later research resolves the identified source, textual, explanatory, and causal deficits rather than treating accumulated study as support for resurrection by itself. Locked score: The strong band reflects substantial motion relevance and meaningful contact with skepticism, while accounting for overstatement, incomplete responsiveness, and the gap between being able to investigate and being warranted in believing.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evidential-deficits-warrant-suspension","time":"111:34","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Unresolved textual problems and report-centered evidence make suspension more reasonable than accepting resurrection as the best explanation of the historical record.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The closing reply aggregates source, textual, and causal concerns into a motion-level conclusion of suspension, preserving the crucial distinction between insufficient warrant and a claim that resurrection is impossible. Principal limitation: Its reference to zero evidence shifts between no surviving physical evidence and no evidence beyond reports, which understates the positive though disputed value of traditions and contextual corroboration. Live burden: The con side must retain the calibrated suspension conclusion while describing opposing evidence accurately and showing why dependence, textual problems, and causal underdetermination outweigh its cumulative contribution. Locked score: The very strong band recognizes excellent relevance, responsive synthesis, and proportionate nonacceptance, while reflecting a limited deduction for absolute evidential phrasing that exceeds the otherwise careful argument.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":80,"strengths":["Landed: The two-part cumulative method clearly separated historical premises from the subsequent comparison of explanations.","Landed: The case offered concrete historical considerations for crucifixion, burial, the empty tomb, appearances, and sincere transformation.","Landed: The defense correctly insisted that ordinary evidence may be considered cumulatively without first proving the extraordinary conclusion.","Landed: The argument expressly addressed bodily meaning and divine causation instead of leaving those necessary bridges entirely implicit."],"blunders":[{"text":"Whiffed: Local detail and witness sincerity were sometimes pressed beyond what they establish, because provenance and conviction do not independently verify resurrection.","links":[]},{"text":"Whiffed: The best-explanation comparison gave natural and combined alternatives less detailed treatment than the preferred resurrection account received.","links":[]},{"text":"Whiffed: The regress objection depended on shifting extraordinary evidence from proportionately strong support to recursively extraordinary claims.","links":[{"label":"Equivocation","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/"}]},{"text":"Whiffed: Religious context and modern investigability did not sufficiently close the separate gaps from unusual event to divine cause and from assessability to warrant.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":86,"strengths":["Landed: The skeptical case consistently separated sincere reports, reported experiences, event truth, mechanism, and divine agency.","Landed: It showed that eliminating selected alternatives does not establish resurrection when the evidential record remains incomplete.","Landed: It clarified the extraordinary-evidence standard as proportionate quantity and quality, decisively answering the regress objection.","Landed: It defended nonacceptance without claiming impossibility, preserving a carefully calibrated burden-based conclusion.","Landed: Source dependence, third-party reporting, textual uncertainty, and causal underdetermination were connected to the motion-level case for suspension."],"blunders":[{"text":"Whiffed: Repeated claims of no or zero evidence understated the disputed but genuine evidential contribution of testimony, tradition, and contextual corroboration.","links":[]},{"text":"Whiffed: Some formulations treated contemporary, physical, or prior supernatural evidence too nearly as categorical prerequisites instead of defensible indicators of evidential strength.","links":[]},{"text":"Whiffed: The natural-alternative comparison identified familiar error mechanisms but did not develop a complete datum-by-datum rival reconstruction.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that resurrection belief may be reasonable, without being compulsory, if several independently supported historical observations are substantially more expected under a bodily-resurrection hypothesis than under the strongest specified natural combinations.","premises":["Historical claims should be graded individually by source date, independence, genre, transmission, and expected preservation, rather than accepted or rejected as one undifferentiated minimal-facts package.","Each surviving datum should contribute only according to how differently resurrection and specified alternatives predict it, with dependent reports discounted so that repeated transmission is not mistaken for independent confirmation.","Transformation and costly commitment support sincere conviction but should not be counted as direct verification; their narrower role is to reduce deliberate conspiracy as one component of a combined alternative.","Divine agency should be inferred only if independently established contextual claims are sufficiently specific, temporally prior, and less expected under ordinary narrative development than under intentional divine action.","Low background frequency should raise the evidential requirement without assigning resurrection zero prior credibility, leaving cumulative ordinary historical evidence eligible to overcome the initial disadvantage in principle."],"conclusion":"If claim-specific source grading, dependence discounts, symmetrical likelihood comparison, and an independently supported causal context jointly favor resurrection by a substantial margin, then accepting it can be epistemically reasonable while suspension remains available to investigators assigning different defensible priors."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Counterfactual data-profile test","text":"A stronger pro case would specify in advance what surviving data would be less expected if resurrection occurred: entirely anonymous late traditions, no early named witnesses, no hostile conversion, and no bodily interpretation near the movement's origin. It would then compare that counterfactual profile with the actual record, applying explicit discounts for dependence and legendary development. This does not prove resurrection, but it turns the argument from a list of favorable facts into a risky comparative model whose failures and successes can be inspected."},{"title":"Convergent-error cost analysis","text":"Natural explanations should be tested both separately and in combinations, but combinations incur coordination costs when distinct mechanisms must align across burial tradition, appearance conviction, bodily interpretation, and early proclamation. The pro argument can compare those costs with the auxiliary assumptions required by resurrection, rather than declaring one side simple by definition. If a natural combination requires several weakly connected errors while resurrection requires fewer independently unsupported additions, that difference may modestly favor the latter, subject to the strong prior disadvantage attached to resurrection."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that suspension is presently more reasonable because uncertain and partly dependent reports do not supply enough discriminating evidence to establish bodily resurrection or identify divine agency, even though resurrection remains logically possible.","premises":["Multiple reports increase confidence only to the extent that they provide independent access to events; shared sources, common traditions, and later editorial development must therefore reduce the apparent multiplicity.","Evidence that early people sincerely reported or interpreted experiences as bodily appearances supports a historical belief state, but the transition from that state to an external resurrected body requires additional discriminating evidence.","A skeptic need not reconstruct every event to withhold belief when several ordinary mechanisms plausibly affect different stages of experience, memory, reporting, transmission, and later synthesis.","Even unusually strong evidence that Jesus was alive after death would establish neither an unspecified supernatural mechanism nor the identity and intention of a divine agent without independent causal indicators.","The testimonial and contextual record should be granted some evidential value, but its force is reduced by contested independence, uncertain preservation, flexible interpretation, and limited capacity to discriminate among causes."],"conclusion":"Because the record provides some support for early resurrection belief but insufficiently discriminates bodily resurrection from layered natural processes or identifies divine causation, withholding acceptance is proportionate and does not entail denial of possibility or occurrence."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Historical information bottleneck","text":"The relevant evidence reaches modern investigators through a narrow transmission bottleneck: a limited set of texts, uncertain access to underlying witnesses, and no repeatable observation of the proposed event. When several hypotheses predict that later communities would preserve confident resurrection reports, adding more detail inside the same transmission network may increase narrative information without adding comparable independent confirmation. This bottleneck does not make historical knowledge impossible, but it limits how decisively the surviving material can discriminate among bodily resurrection, visionary experience, memory development, and theological synthesis."},{"title":"Causal identification ladder","text":"The inquiry should proceed through distinct evidential levels: a person was reported dead, followers later believed he appeared, an external living body was observed, an unknown mechanism restored life, a supernatural cause operated, and a particular God intentionally acted. Evidence sufficient for an earlier level does not automatically establish the next. Mapping every source claim onto this ladder reveals where support is comparatively strong and where it becomes sparse. The method strengthens skepticism by locating the precise inferential shortfall without dismissing the historical evidence that supports lower levels."}]}},"year":2019};
